emy-id "q-109418" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/f70f186e51a312b26dc4e2cfa3a4d812fab6583ada8b8af82f153a9975948b4b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x)=2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+{(2x-3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-12x+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109418/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109418"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-104518"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261/selection/62128986b44a94a943f431b0a8c72c7bce66d61f83a7d41f531a8bdd166e5d50" :answer/id #uuid "b302ff15-c1f7-51c3-b651-b19250f131a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261/selection/aefb84891d16f547861bc58b82248fbdd35ef5e6df7a4b84c530832cabd454b5" :answer/id #uuid "4e3720d8-e838-5615-b913-b130824fb4fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261/selection/e658b7fcdde15758dfebc50d374e8daa7bfbcc011e4a3559e4817ba895d24d4a" :answer/id #uuid "04552900-9000-58d5-b3f8-9d76d9a911ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261/selection/9571dd21d1392db6d7852baa566246ccad3589385a87b081b3fae8bc307d40dd" :answer/id #uuid "b0fa417d-66ae-51e0-8669-8b320b43f56c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261/selection/cb2977914eee2fc7798a3c38d6163a9339bcbbc5d4155b43590988db918f9253" :answer/id #uuid "22eba780-b2ca-53b9-be8a-d45de50a6808" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed22e06-904d-5d92-a07f-2c0dbe381873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109261/selection/9571dd21d1392db6d7852baa566246ccad3589385a87b081b3fae8bc307d40dd" :answer-field/choices ["q-109261/selection/62128986b44a94a943f431b0a8c72c7bce66d61f83a7d41f531a8bdd166e5d50" "q-109261/selection/aefb84891d16f547861bc58b82248fbdd35ef5e6df7a4b84c530832cabd454b5" "q-109261/selection/e658b7fcdde15758dfebc50d374e8daa7bfbcc011e4a3559e4817ba895d24d4a" "q-109261/selection/9571dd21d1392db6d7852baa566246ccad3589385a87b081b3fae8bc307d40dd" "q-109261/selection/cb2977914eee2fc7798a3c38d6163a9339bcbbc5d4155b43590988db918f9253"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109261" :question/id #uuid "0bee3dde-bc52-5306-8b97-caa53ff33030" :question/math-academy-id "q-109261" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109261/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109261"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420/selection/6fefcec27b3a55d89b253986ae09f588e6882af9d92a34e2d694a722c0f2e7de" :answer/id #uuid "e8e7f0af-1c4a-5c10-bbcc-be0a9a8479d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420/selection/212f44f5a35c713e60fe114775db3dbd411b86dba8f70b721701e277a8026f99" :answer/id #uuid "88cf1bf7-9bae-5fd3-ac8e-26d588695123" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420/selection/b2b6e6172e9470d1fc096cfdccbfe4fda9ed0c1dc15324f89b70fed4a5acaccc" :answer/id #uuid "c55a2487-082b-5340-b298-150089224ff1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{12-5{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420/selection/95fe5074e6a141f3c5d290d69abe09a0ea43223c3dd7d13af59dbad10129da40" :answer/id #uuid "8b5cb24c-20de-57d6-b730-0c78a6a0a232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{4-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420/selection/f3d7b8d06b6a311bfde0e53549b10f6614cda4d7054082154f0382f5b0f04d8b" :answer/id #uuid "c2096800-1d98-5370-b94f-74087a96247b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{4-3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "48a7915a-ba9f-5b5c-a5d0-afced806b268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109420/selection/212f44f5a35c713e60fe114775db3dbd411b86dba8f70b721701e277a8026f99" :answer-field/choices ["q-109420/selection/6fefcec27b3a55d89b253986ae09f588e6882af9d92a34e2d694a722c0f2e7de" "q-109420/selection/212f44f5a35c713e60fe114775db3dbd411b86dba8f70b721701e277a8026f99" "q-109420/selection/b2b6e6172e9470d1fc096cfdccbfe4fda9ed0c1dc15324f89b70fed4a5acaccc" "q-109420/selection/95fe5074e6a141f3c5d290d69abe09a0ea43223c3dd7d13af59dbad10129da40" "q-109420/selection/f3d7b8d06b6a311bfde0e53549b10f6614cda4d7054082154f0382f5b0f04d8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109420" :question/id #uuid "5c20af06-f742-5d0f-a68e-3d8868398167" :question/math-academy-id "q-109420" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/21d8b516e09e592bd77c815ba50b10faed648386013f9d0a618df5ec758b9a91.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=5x-{x}^{3}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x(5-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=\\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-{x}^{3})=5-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{1+{(5-3{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{5}}x\\sqrt{26-30{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109420/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109420"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353019/field-1/4767e71c203b8ccf237614e08dd6805392e2d9a296c16ada75abffc82df102ee" :answer/id #uuid "6db26732-f885-507c-9d0c-db8fbff8f515" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353019/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba67a48c-6d26-5da6-a7f3-9708b4bd01af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353019/field-1/4767e71c203b8ccf237614e08dd6805392e2d9a296c16ada75abffc82df102ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-353019/field-1/4767e71c203b8ccf237614e08dd6805392e2d9a296c16ada75abffc82df102ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353019" :question/id #uuid "05455a71-2acd-54af-8ede-155df1478797" :question/math-academy-id "q-353019" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=1+{x}^{2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+{x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+4{x}^{2}.$ Then, $8x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}x\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(2)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-353019/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353019"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-104529"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y}{2\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi \\,y{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415/selection/c3c4f1bafee7abbd7afc920121c744e7e0c25607dfa037bcb9aeda502cb8afc8" :answer/id #uuid "71a59533-649b-5d91-8a27-4f9162b5b1a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{20\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415/selection/b75f6b831447a7f0b25896b29d5b16f6065b6b04851f7854c39041781c8b77ef" :answer/id #uuid "ce6392b6-fca6-5949-a216-7c009590ac95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415/selection/9c0fa8883b404bb04df19f435f40eea5eddcd008f7af635264fff01ed535c469" :answer/id #uuid "63f6f98d-7175-5b60-ad1a-7a3aec351e6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415/selection/d445dc8a1d5f227405f4a6e30b25d8dddd08eb278ea33e6c3dc3c9de212c71fc" :answer/id #uuid "931e159e-fab9-5f02-9c4a-e1e82b238a68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415/selection/531bb39fab2575ffb2ae4e10b0292f5b9b93ea7106f7ae91bd1b69e8875af00e" :answer/id #uuid "b207a69d-4d85-5560-826b-642021578a3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi \\sqrt[3]{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "94571208-ec13-5b20-bda6-923048467fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109415/selection/9c0fa8883b404bb04df19f435f40eea5eddcd008f7af635264fff01ed535c469" :answer-field/choices ["q-109415/selection/c3c4f1bafee7abbd7afc920121c744e7e0c25607dfa037bcb9aeda502cb8afc8" "q-109415/selection/b75f6b831447a7f0b25896b29d5b16f6065b6b04851f7854c39041781c8b77ef" "q-109415/selection/9c0fa8883b404bb04df19f435f40eea5eddcd008f7af635264fff01ed535c469" "q-109415/selection/d445dc8a1d5f227405f4a6e30b25d8dddd08eb278ea33e6c3dc3c9de212c71fc" "q-109415/selection/531bb39fab2575ffb2ae4e10b0292f5b9b93ea7106f7ae91bd1b69e8875af00e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109415" :question/id #uuid "9bc19c87-6706-5df0-8c49-f02af32af50e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109415" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y={(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{1/2}(-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/3}{(4-{x}^{2/3})}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}={x}^{-2/3}(4-{x}^{2/3})=4{x}^{-2/3}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+(4{x}^{-2/3}-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{4{x}^{-2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {[\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-109415/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109415"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731/selection/26734acc4349599b013ff5890611204a590e31e398b69a9ba8c4c553e3de6e98" :answer/id #uuid "20797785-32e4-5473-b653-54ee0b13660d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731/selection/5ec4a600c18064fc4549df0159725429ba4c31c98adda0c18b1ff572ab2074bc" :answer/id #uuid "853ad045-c621-5a92-bc48-87219614fd25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731/selection/7acf7b5bc5fc24bd812fd8af1006782fdd8032ab5554b7ec7c2db46336c22ae2" :answer/id #uuid "be6483c6-5e1c-547d-a90c-e407e4b139b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731/selection/35d0af53e25a7911f6d08c6d063fcf9231a1273a16a0f7056530d6f5d5dfbdd8" :answer/id #uuid "741818e3-f923-5151-899e-b84a557211a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731/selection/ded5223ba109b16050fd2ed9a98315e4fa4e8b74b34949bbeaad746173ab22ec" :answer/id #uuid "0739faf7-dff8-544c-901c-028c60753eec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "63b38dc9-25bc-5057-a2c1-310aed9d4497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126731/selection/ded5223ba109b16050fd2ed9a98315e4fa4e8b74b34949bbeaad746173ab22ec" :answer-field/choices ["q-126731/selection/26734acc4349599b013ff5890611204a590e31e398b69a9ba8c4c553e3de6e98" "q-126731/selection/5ec4a600c18064fc4549df0159725429ba4c31c98adda0c18b1ff572ab2074bc" "q-126731/selection/7acf7b5bc5fc24bd812fd8af1006782fdd8032ab5554b7ec7c2db46336c22ae2" "q-126731/selection/35d0af53e25a7911f6d08c6d063fcf9231a1273a16a0f7056530d6f5d5dfbdd8" "q-126731/selection/ded5223ba109b16050fd2ed9a98315e4fa4e8b74b34949bbeaad746173ab22ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126731" :question/id #uuid "bdbf3732-d9db-5aa9-97e3-008429e155d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126731" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\sqrt[4]{3}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}={y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}{y}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{y}^{4}.$ Then, ${y}^{3}\\text{d}y=(\\frac{1}{4})\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{3}}{y}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126731/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126731"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-109413"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2})={x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=x.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{2(4)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-(\\frac{2(1)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{116}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{232\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8907"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^2$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown below. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/30c2b0000055802166e5bdf8ec3e305d83e65ac960043f66463702952d91e4d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9121"] :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x^3-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14082799/assets/777c36689da6f6cbcf4ce92a7c5ba17c000a7c442a972851d9d1cafaa9fb2ba4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x= \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-3x)=3{x}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+(3{x}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}-18{x}^{2}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8906"] :question/problem "Consider the segment of the line $y = 2x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this segment is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x\\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
